## 1. The change (at the cause, not a suppression)

`packages/compiler/reflaxe/swift/swiftcompiler/SwiftExpr.hx`:

- `blockLines` — the one funnel that lowers every function-body statement
  list — now tracks a local `diverged` flag and **stops emitting** statements
  that follow one which diverges.
- New `stmtDiverges(e)`: conservative local judgement — `TReturn`, `TThrow`,
  a block ending in either, an `if` with both arms diverging, a `try` whose
  body and every catch diverge, and a **variant switch judged with exactly the
  knowledge the switch lowering itself uses** (`enumTable`, the constant
  pattern indices, exhaustive coverage, and the arms' own bodies). Anything
  unproven returns `false`, so a statement is skipped only when the emitter
  itself would render a Swift switch/throw whose successor is dead. The
  judgement lives in the same function that emits the text, so the emitted
  text and the emitter's reachability knowledge cannot disagree.

No `@Suppress`-style marker, no attribute, no change to what any program
computes (§4). The dead statement is simply not emitted.

The previous stopped attempt (`w2-diagnostic-fix`) has no hash; nothing of it
was reused — this was written afresh from its recorded intent
(`GATE-LEDGER.md`, ruling files).

